<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
                            http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.25.xsd">

    <!--
        Retires `pos.order.void`, a permission row that exists in this database and is declared by
        no changeset anywhere.

        It was applied by hand. Nothing enforces it: POS voids authorize on
        hasAnyAuthority('pos.order.void.own','pos.order.void.any'), and the Rego policies read the
        same two codes. The bare code is therefore a permission that grants nothing while looking
        like it grants everything about voiding — the worst shape a stale grant can take, because a
        reviewer reading role_permissions concludes MANAGER can void and cannot tell from which row.

        It also spreads. Changesets 030/041/042 grant OWNER and TENANT_ADMIN *every* row in
        `permissions` via INSERT ... SELECT code FROM permissions, so an orphan row is picked up by
        those blanket grants on any database where it exists — which is why the drift showed up
        against three roles rather than the one it was inserted for.

        Scope note: an earlier pass reported ~46 undeclared grants. That count was a measurement
        error — it parsed only literal VALUES tuples and so missed the SELECT-based blanket grants
        above, which ARE declared. Re-measured properly, the drift is this single permission code
        and the three role rows it produced. A fresh deployment matches this database once it is
        gone.

        Deletes rather than declares: adding it to the catalog would bless a code no authorization
        path consults. Idempotent and safe on a clean database, where it deletes nothing.
    -->
    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-054-retire-orphan-pos-order-void" author="restaurantos">
        <comment>Remove the hand-applied pos.order.void permission; .own/.any are what enforce</comment>

        <sql>
            DELETE FROM role_permissions WHERE permission_code = 'pos.order.void';
        </sql>
        <sql>
            DELETE FROM permissions WHERE code = 'pos.order.void';
        </sql>

        <rollback>
            <!--
                Restoring the orphan means restoring a row no changeset declares, so the rollback
                recreates exactly what was removed and nothing more: the catalog row plus the three
                role grants observed on 2026-08-02 (MANAGER directly; OWNER and TENANT_ADMIN via
                the blanket grants).
            -->
            <sql>
                INSERT INTO permissions (code, name, description)
                VALUES ('pos.order.void', 'Void order', 'Superseded by pos.order.void.own/.any')
                ON CONFLICT (code) DO NOTHING;
            </sql>
            <sql>
                INSERT INTO role_permissions (role_code, permission_code) VALUES
                    ('MANAGER', 'pos.order.void'),
                    ('OWNER', 'pos.order.void'),
                    ('TENANT_ADMIN', 'pos.order.void')
                ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
